  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Given a Secure Record BU with exactly one named non-default owner team with zero members and zero BU users, provisioning a secure sprk_project, sprk_matter and sprk_workassignment each ends with the row owned by that team (read-back verified), the caller shared at creator rights, and the row's own container recorded.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">NEGATIVE: the named team has one member (human OR application user) — provisioning refuses with a new reason code before any mutation; the record is unchanged.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">NEGATIVE: any systemuser (enabled or disabled) has `businessunitid` = the Secure Record BU — provisioning refuses with a new reason code before any mutation.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">NEGATIVE: the named team is missing, or two teams match — refuses; the default team is NEVER selected (a fixture roster containing the default team plus decoys proves it).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">NEGATIVE: the membership or BU-user read throws — refuses (fail closed), not "zero".</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">AUTHORIZATION: the delegation filter authorizes the SAME root the handler re-owns for all three entity types; a caller without Write on that record gets 403 and nothing changes; an unknown RecordType is refused.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Unsecure works for sprk_matter and sprk_workassignment exactly as for sprk_project (reassign, read-back, share sweep, flag clear, sweepComplete reported); the legacy ProjectId request shape still works for both routes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">RecordOwnershipResolver: a target owned in the Secure Record BU resolves to the named team; a target in any other BU still resolves to that BU's default team; an unresolvable named team returns null (refuse), never the default team.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">NEGATIVE: registration refuses to create a systemuser whose configured BU resolves (by ID, in the target environment) to the Secure Record BU — no systemuser row is created; an unreadable Secure BU lookup also refuses; a user whose BU is any other BU is created as today.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">SecureBuRoleDepthAssertion: clause 2 targets the named team and fails naming any member; the no-user-BU clause fails naming any user in the BU; clause 3 fails if the owner role is on the default team. Each clause is seeded both ways in unit tests.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">MANUAL LIVE GATE (dev): after migration, `sprk_issecure eq true` rows on all three roots are owned by the named team (0 by the default team); POA share counts per row are equal before and after; the default team holds no role; the NFR-05 assertion passes; the guide §7 impersonated isolation probe (existing non-admin test user) is DENIED on a secure project, a secure matter and a secure work assignment. Both outputs recorded in the task note.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Migration: the dry run writes nothing; a second -Apply run changes nothing.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Guide §4 and §7 and the write-path I-2 row describe the named team, the zero-member and zero-user invariants, and the default team's retirement.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">BFF unit and integration suites green; NetArchTest green; publish size reported per CLAUDE.md §10 and at most 60 MB; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Test scope: the stated contract plus the negative cases named above. Anything beyond needs a one-line justification in the task note.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Defect C10 half 1 (session26 note, C10) and owner round 2 item 6 (session27 note). Hole 2 (BU depth) is discovered scope: the original defect framed the risk as default-team membership only, but a named team does not stop a user in the Secure BU with BU-depth roles. The C10 part-2 work (child re-own, backfill, client-created children, contact coverage) and task 145 (owner-role child privileges, #1046) build on the named team this task introduces. Task 150 (sprk_issecure lock) changes the same provisioning endpoint and depends on this task. Cite #967 in the PR; it closes here.</notes>

  <execution>
    <skill>.claude/skills/task-execute/SKILL.md</skill>
    <protocol>Load all files in knowledge before starting. The live cutover in step 9 is prescriptive and runs only after the code is deployed to the dev BFF.</protocol>
    <outcome date="2026-10-01" branch="task/uac-r2-144">
      COMPLETED-WITH-ESCALATION: code, tests, docs and the migration script are done. The live cutover (step 9) is a
      PENDING MANUAL GATE: this run was read-only against Dataverse by instruction, and three owner decisions block it.
      Full record: notes/task-144-named-secure-owner-team.md.

      CODE.
      (1) Provisioning (ProvisionProjectEndpoint + new static SecureRecordOwnerTeam) owns secure roots by the Secure Record BU's
          NAMED, non-default Owner team (SecureRecord:OwnerTeamName, default "Secure Record Owners", owner decision F9),
          resolved by BU id + name + teamtype 0 + isdefault false, $top=2, exactly one. It is never the default team.
          Before any mutation it proves zero teammembership rows of ANY kind and zero systemusers (any kind) in the BU.
          An unreadable answer to either refuses. New reason codes: secure_owner_team_has_members,
          secure_owner_team_membership_unreadable, secure_bu_has_users, secure_bu_users_unreadable. A record still owned by
          another team inside the BU (the retired default team) returns 409 owned_by_other_secure_team and is pointed at
          the migration script, not given a second container.
      (2) Provision and unsecure accept recordType + recordId (project | matter | workassignment; the grant routes'
          vocabulary). The legacy projectId still works. Per-entity sets, columns and container labels come from ONE
          table (SecureRecordRoot, which wraps ExternalGrantRoot).
      (3) DelegationRuleFilter resolves the SAME root as the handler (SecureRecordRoot.ResolveTarget → ResolveGrantRoot).
          An unknown recordType gets 403.
      (4) RecordOwnershipResolver maps the Secure Record BU → its named team, or null (refuse). It never returns the
          default team. An ambiguous Secure BU name refuses. With no Secure BU in the environment, every BU keeps its
          default team.
      (5) Registration refuses, BY ID and in the target environment, to create a systemuser in the Secure BU, and to add
          a member to ANY team inside it (a discovered vector). An unreadable or ambiguous lookup refuses with
          SecureRecordPlacementRefusedException before any POST.
